  "body": "## Bug report\r\n\r\n- Operating System: Ubuntu 20.04\r\n- ROS2 Version: Galactic binaries\r\n- Version or commit hash: Galactic 1.0.12-1focal.20220526.142755\r\n- DDS implementation: Cyclone DDS (Galactic default)\r\n\r\n#### Steps to reproduce issue\r\nSend a goal using RViz 2D Nav Goal (or send a single goal that has a non-zero timestamp in its header) to a running Nav2 stack.\r\n\r\n#### Expected behavior\r\nRegardless of the amount of time the robot will spend traveling to the goal, the goal pose should remain valid.\r\n\r\n#### Actual behavior\r\nIf the path takes longer to complete than navigation2/costmap_2d_ros's internal tf buffer, path planning will fail when the `planner_server` attempts to transform the goal pose into the correct frame when it tries to `computePlanThroughPoses` (which will call `costmap_2d_ros::transformPoseToGlobalFrame`).\r\n\r\n#### Additional information\r\nIt is possible that this is not a bug, and instead desired behavior. If this is intentional, what is the use case for a goal with a timestamp that will eventually fall outside of the tf buffer?",
